C=[];Q=[]
def case(id,title,system,src,obj,scen,ids,expl,steps,esc,qs):
    C.append(dict(id=id,title=title,system=system,src=src,objective=obj,scenario=scen,relevantStructureIds=ids,explanation=expl,management=steps,escalate=esc,questionIds=[]))
    for k,q in enumerate(qs,1):
        qid=f"q-em-{id}-{k}"
        q=dict(q);q['id']=qid;q['topic']=system;q['src']=src;q['related']=ids[:3];C[-1]['questionIds'].append(qid);Q.append(q)
def mc(prompt,opts,ans,expl,diff='intermediate',obj='Link anatomy to emergency care'):
    return dict(type='multiple-choice',prompt=prompt,options=opts,correct='abcd'[ans],explanation=expl,difficulty=diff,objective=obj)
def tf(prompt,ans,expl,diff='foundation',obj='Link anatomy to emergency care'):
    return dict(type='true-false',prompt=prompt,options=['True','False'],correct='a' if ans else 'b',explanation=expl,difficulty=diff,objective=obj)
# ---------------- cardiovascular
case('em-heart-attack','Chest pain and a suspected heart attack','cardiovascular','heart',
 'Relate the coronary arteries to the territories of a heart attack and list the first-response steps.',
 'A 58-year-old man has had crushing central chest pressure for 20 minutes. It spreads to the left arm and jaw, and he is sweaty and pale.',
 ['heart','coronary-arteries','left-ventricle','aorta'],
 'The right and left coronary arteries arise from the aortic root and supply the heart muscle. A blood clot on a ruptured fatty plaque blocks flow, and the muscle beyond it starts to die within minutes. The left anterior descending artery supplies most of the front of the left ventricle and the front of the septum; the right coronary artery supplies the inferior wall and usually the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes. Pain is carried by sympathetic visceral afferents (T1-T4) and is therefore felt in the chest, left arm and jaw.',
 ['Call the emergency number straight away. Do not drive the person yourself.','Sit the person down in a comfortable position (half-sitting, knees bent) and keep them calm and still.','If the person is not allergic and has no reason to avoid it (for example bleeding or a doctor\'s instruction), give one adult aspirin (300 mg) to chew slowly, as advised by the emergency operator.','Loosen tight clothing and stay with them. Ask someone to fetch an automated external defibrillator (AED) if one is available.','If they become unresponsive and are not breathing normally, start CPR and use the AED (see the cardiac arrest case).'],
 'Every episode of chest pain that lasts more than a few minutes, or comes with sweating, breathlessness, nausea or faintness, is an emergency.',
 [mc('Which coronary artery supplies most of the front of the left ventricle?',['Right marginal artery','Left anterior descending artery','Posterior interventricular artery','Sinoatrial nodal artery'],1,'The left anterior descending (anterior interventricular) artery runs in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior left ventricle and the anterior two-thirds of the septum.','foundation','Name the coronary arteries and their territories'),
  mc('What is the first action when someone has suspected heart-attack chest pain?',['Give them a large meal','Ask them to walk to the car','Call the emergency number and keep the person resting','Wait to see if the pain goes away'],2,'Early call for help is the most important step. The person should rest, and treatment can start before reaching hospital.','foundation','List first-response steps')])
case('em-cardiac-arrest','Collapse and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)','cardiovascular','heart',
 'Explain where chest compressions are applied and why, and describe the adult CPR sequence.',
 'A 62-year-old woman collapses in a shop. She does not respond when shaken and is not breathing normally (only occasional gasps).',
 ['heart','sternum','right-ventricle','left-ventricle'],
 'The heart lies behind the lower half of the sternum, slightly to the left, between the sternum and the vertebral column. Pressing down on the lower half of the sternum squeezes the heart and moves blood to the brain and organs. Brain cells begin to be damaged within minutes without blood flow, so compressions must start at once and be interrupted as little as possible.',
 ['Check that the scene is safe. Shake the shoulders and shout. If there is no response, shout for help and call the emergency number (or send someone) and ask for an AED.','Open the airway (head tilt and chin lift) and look for normal breathing for no more than 10 seconds. Occasional gasps are not normal breathing.','Start chest compressions: heel of one hand on the centre of the chest (lower half of the sternum), other hand on top, arms straight. Push down about 5-6 cm at 100-120 compressions per minute and let the chest recoil fully.','If trained, give 2 rescue breaths after every 30 compressions. If not trained or unwilling, give continuous compressions only.','As soon as the AED arrives, switch it on and follow its voice prompts. Do not touch the person while it analyses or shocks. Continue until help takes over, the person starts to breathe normally or you are too exhausted to continue.'],
 'Call the emergency number for every person who is unresponsive and not breathing normally.',
 [mc('Where are chest compressions given in an adult?',['Upper part of the sternum','Left side of the chest over the apex','Centre of the chest on the lower half of the sternum','Upper abdomen below the ribs'],2,'The heart lies under the lower half of the sternum, so compressions are given in the centre of the chest.','foundation','Locate the compression point'),
  tf('Occasional gasps in an unresponsive person are normal breathing, so CPR is not needed.',False,'False. Agonal gasps are common in the first minutes of cardiac arrest and are not normal breathing. Start CPR.','foundation','Recognise cardiac arrest')])
case('em-severe-bleeding','Severe bleeding from a limb','cardiovascular','vessels',
 'Relate the course of the main limb arteries to the steps used to control life-threatening bleeding.',
 'A worker cuts his thigh deeply with a power tool. Bright red blood is spurting from the wound and he is becoming pale and drowsy.',
 ['femoral-artery','brachial-artery','radial-artery','popliteal-artery'],
 'The femoral artery passes under the inguinal ligament and runs down the front of the thigh in the femoral triangle, then continues as the popliteal artery. Deep wounds can cut large arteries, and the blood pressure makes blood spurt. A person can lose a dangerous volume within minutes. The brachial artery in the arm and the femoral artery in the thigh run close to bone, so pressure against bone can slow the flow.',
 ['Call the emergency number. Put on gloves if available.','Press firmly directly on the wound with a clean pad or cloth, using your whole hand. Do not remove the pad if it soaks through: add more and keep pressing.','If bleeding from an arm or leg cannot be controlled and the person is in danger, apply a tourniquet 5-7 cm above the wound (not over a joint), tighten until bleeding stops, note the time, and do not loosen it.','Lay the person down, keep them warm, and raise the legs if there is no fracture. Do not give food or drink.','Watch for shock (pale, cold, fast pulse, confusion). Be ready to start CPR if they stop breathing.'],
 'Any bleeding that spurts, soaks through dressings, or comes with pallor, faintness or confusion needs emergency help.',
 [mc('Which artery runs down the front of the thigh in the femoral triangle?',['Popliteal artery','Femoral artery','Anterior tibial artery','External iliac vein'],1,'The femoral artery, the continuation of the external iliac artery under the inguinal ligament, runs in the femoral triangle.','foundation','Locate the femoral artery'),
  mc('What is the first step to control severe external bleeding?',['Apply direct pressure to the wound','Remove any dressing that has soaked through','Wash the wound with water','Lift the person to stand'],0,'Firm direct pressure is the first and most important step. Add more pads over the first one rather than removing it.','foundation','State the first step')])
# ---------------- respiratory
case('em-choking','Choking on food','respiratory','respiratory',
 'Relate the anatomy of the airway to the first-aid steps for an adult who is choking.',
 'A man at a restaurant suddenly grasps his throat and cannot speak, cough or breathe.',
 ['larynx','epiglottis','trachea','right-main-bronchus'],
 'Food passes through the pharynx and can slip into the laryngeal inlet, which the epiglottis normally covers during swallowing. A solid object lodged in the larynx or trachea blocks the airway completely. Smaller objects pass lower and enter the right main bronchus more often, because it is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left. Coughing is the most effective way to clear a partial blockage.',
 ['If the person can cough, speak or breathe, encourage them to keep coughing and do not hit them on the back.','If the blockage is severe (cannot speak, cough or breathe): lean the person forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand.','If this does not work, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: stand behind, put your arms around the abdomen, make a fist just above the navel, grasp it with your other hand and pull sharply inwards and upwards.','Alternate 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts. Call the emergency number if the blockage is not cleared quickly.','If the person becomes unresponsive, lay them down, call for help and begin CPR. Check the mouth for a visible object before giving breaths.','For a baby (under 1 year): give 5 back blows with the baby face down along your forearm, then 5 chest thrusts (two fingers on the lower half of the sternum). Do not use abdominal thrusts.'],
 'Anyone who has had abdominal thrusts must be seen by a doctor afterwards, even if they seem well.',
 [mc('Which main bronchus is more commonly entered by an inhaled object?',['Left main bronchus, because it is wider','Right main bronchus, because it is wider and more vertical','Both are equally likely','Neither; objects stay in the trachea'],1,'The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left.','foundation','Explain airway anatomy and aspiration'),
  mc('For an adult with a severe airway blockage who is still conscious, which sequence is taught first?',['Mouth-to-mouth breaths','5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts','Chest compressions only','Give water to drink'],1,'The standard sequence is 5 back blows between the shoulder blades followed by 5 abdominal thrusts, repeated.','foundation','State the first-aid sequence')])
case('em-pneumothorax','Stab wound to the chest and a collapsing lung','respiratory','respiratory',
 'Explain how air in the pleural space collapses the lung and describe the first response, including the safe site for needle decompression.',
 'A young man is stabbed in the right side of the chest. He is struggling to breathe, there is a hissing wound, and his neck veins are swollen.',
 ['pleura','right-lung','ribs','trachea'],
 'The lungs are held to the chest wall by the thin film of fluid between the visceral and parietal pleura. If air enters this space through the chest wall or a torn lung, the lung collapses. In a tension pneumothorax a one-way valve effect traps more air with each breath, pushing the mediastinum to the opposite side and kinking the great veins, so blood pressure falls. The intercostal vein, artery and nerve run under the lower border of each rib, so a needle is passed over the upper border of the rib below the chosen space.',
 ['Call the emergency number. Do not remove any object stuck in the chest.','Cover an open wound with a dressing that stops air entering but lets air out (a commercial vented chest seal, or a clean square of plastic taped on three sides). Keep the person half-sitting.','Give oxygen if available and trained. Watch breathing, colour and pulse.','Clinician-level treatment of tension pneumothorax: needle or finger decompression in the 4th-5th intercostal space in the mid-axillary or anterior axillary line (an alternative is the 2nd space in the mid-clavicular line), passing over the top of the rib, then chest drain insertion.'],
 'A chest wound, severe breathlessness, or a rapidly worsening person needs emergency care immediately.',
 [mc('Why is a needle passed over the upper border of a rib?',['The rib is thinner there','The intercostal vein, artery and nerve run under the lower border of the rib above','The pleura is thicker there','The lung lies higher there'],1,'The neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove under the lower border of each rib.','intermediate','Locate the intercostal neurovascular bundle'),
  tf('In a tension pneumothorax the trachea is typically pushed towards the affected side.',False,'False. The mediastinum and trachea are pushed away from the affected side.','intermediate','Recognise tension pneumothorax')])
case('em-asthma','Severe asthma attack','respiratory','respiratory',
 'Explain how narrowing of the bronchioles causes wheeze, and describe the first-aid steps.',
 'A 12-year-old with known asthma is wheezing, cannot finish a sentence and is using the muscles of the neck to breathe.',
 ['bronchial-tree','bronchioles','right-lung','left-lung'],
 'The bronchi and bronchioles have smooth muscle in their walls. In asthma the muscle tightens, the lining swells and mucus is produced, so the airways narrow, mostly during breathing out, producing a wheeze. Parasympathetic fibres of the vagus nerve cause constriction and sympathetic fibres (beta-2 receptors) cause dilation. Reliever inhalers act as beta-2 agonists and relax the muscle.',
 ['Sit the person upright and keep calm. Do not lie them down.','Help them use the reliever inhaler (usually blue) through a spacer if they have one, following their written action plan. A usual first-aid instruction is one puff at a time, taking about five breaths after each, up to 10 puffs.','Call the emergency number if the symptoms are severe, if the person is getting worse, if they cannot speak in full sentences, or if there is no improvement after the reliever.','While waiting, repeat the reliever as instructed in their plan and stay with them.','If they become exhausted, drowsy, or stop breathing normally, begin CPR.'],
 'A silent chest, blue lips, exhaustion or confusion are signs of a life-threatening attack.',
 [mc('Which structures narrow in an asthma attack?',['Alveoli','Bronchi and bronchioles','Pulmonary veins','Larynx only'],1,'Smooth-muscle spasm, swelling and mucus narrow the bronchi and bronchioles.','foundation','Relate asthma to airway anatomy'),
  mc('Which nerve supplies the parasympathetic fibres that cause bronchoconstriction?',['Phrenic nerve','Vagus nerve','Sympathetic trunk','Glossopharyngeal nerve'],1,'The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibres to the airway smooth muscle.','intermediate','Name the autonomic supply of the airways')])
